{
  "coordinated_changes": {
    "ADR-035-workstream-id-signed-field": {
      "record": "docs/adrs/ADR-035-cycle-is-the-unit-of-parallelism.md",
      "identifiers": [
        "workstream_id",
        "WorkstreamID"
      ],
      "reason": "`workstream_id` is a key in the signed envelope body, and what makes it undroppable is presence rather than requirement. It is not in `_ENVELOPE_REQUIRED_STR`; it is in `_ENVELOPE_PRESENT_STR` (`core/lib/runtime_contracts.py`), which demands the key even when the value is empty, `build_envelope` always emits it, and `ENVELOPE_SIGNED_FIELDS` lists it. So the control plane has signed a payload containing that key on every envelope it has ever issued, and a verifier that drops it from its list hashes a different byte string from the one signed: not the next envelope written, but all of them, including those already issued. ADR-035 records the consequence, that removing the field is one coordinated versioned contract change across envelope, signature, transport, receipt, schema and UI, not a field deletion. The signed-field list is hand-kept in three places (`core/lib/runtime_contracts.py`, `api/synaptory_api/envelope_signing.py`, `cli/internal/cpclient/envelope_verify.go`), so the drift guard between the copies catches a divergence and cannot see a removal made in all three at once, which is exactly what discharging one of these rows would look like to a careful sweeper. Therefore no row in this group is discharged on its own: the field leaves every file in the group in one release or it leaves none of them. Part of it is irreversible, because `workstream_id` attribution on `subagent_spans` and `gate_events` has no payload fallback and is archived before the columns are dropped."
    }
  },
